      "description": "Watch Burnley vs Middlesbrough highlights and goals from Championship on ReFooty: Football Highlights Hub. Match played on September 2, 2026. The atmosphere at Turf Moor was electric as Burnley hosted Middlesbrough in a highly anticipated Championship clash. Both sides looked to establish their dominance early on, setting the stage for a tactical and physical battle in front of a passionate crowd. Middlesbrough Strike First On September 2, 2026 , the match began with high energy, but it was the visitors who found the clinical edge first. In the 23rd minute, Sebastian Berhalter turned provider, delivering a precise ball to Aidan Morris , who made no mistake in finding the back of the net. The opening goal forced Burnley to chase the game, leading to some heated exchanges on the pitch. Hannibal Mejbri was the first to enter the referee's book, receiving a yellow card in the 28th minute as the home side struggled to contain the Middlesbrough momentum. As the first half drew to a close, the physical nature of the contest continued. Middlesbrough forward Will Lankshear received a yellow card in the 39th minute for a mistimed challenge. Despite several half-chances for the hosts, the Boro defense held firm, taking their slim lead into the halftime break. The Turf Moor Fightback Burnley emerged for the second half with renewed intent, making a double substitution as Max Alleyne and Largie Ramazani entered the fray. The tactical shift paid dividends in the 54th minute. Zeki Amdouni showed great awareness to set up Ugo Raghouber , who unleashed a powerful strike to level the score. The equalizer shifted the momentum entirely, with the home fans roaring their approval as Burnley pushed for a winner. A Tense Conclusion The final stages of the match were defined by tactical changes and defensive discipline. Middlesbrough 's Alfie Jones saw yellow in the 71st minute during a period of sustained pressure from the hosts. Both managers utilized their benches extensively, with Armando Broja and David Strelec being introduced late on to provide fresh legs. In the dying moments of stoppage time, Ugo Raghouber received a yellow card, a testament to the uncompromising nature of the duel until the very end. The referee blew the final whistle at 90+4', concluding a hard-fought encounter where neither side could find the decisive final blow."
